  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: author.name,
    section: "Brand Reviews",
    tags: [
      "nutricost whey protein concentrate powder",
      "nutricost casein protein powder",
      "nutricost protein powder",
      "nutricost whey protein isolate",
      "is nutricost protein good"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{
    text: "See the Cheapest Protein Powders Ranked",
    link: "/best-cheap-protein-powder/",
  }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker={"Brand Review"} badge={"Label-Checked"} author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">Nutricost Protein Powder Review</Fragment>
      A budget brand with plain labels and low prices. Here is which Nutricost tub to buy, what it really costs per 25 g of protein, and what its testing claim does and does not cover.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#short-verdict">The Short Verdict</a>
      <a href="#lineup">What Nutricost Sells</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">Our Top Picks</a>
      <a href="#label">The Whey Concentrate Label</a>
      <a href="#cost">Cost per 25 g, With the Math</a>
      <a href="#isolate-casein-pea">Isolate, Casein and Pea</a>
      <a href="#testing">Third-Party Testing</a>
      <a href="#taste">Taste and Mixing</a>
      <a href="#who-should-skip">Who Should Skip It</a>
      <a href="#rivals">Nutricost vs the Rivals</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          Nutricost sells some of the cheapest name-brand protein on Amazon, and for once the cheap option is not a trick. The unflavored whey concentrate is one ingredient plus a little sunflower lecithin, 25 g of protein per scoop per the label, at about $1.42 per 25 g of protein at the price we saw. The surprise is lower down the range: the casein and the whey isolate are even better value per gram than the flagship concentrate. What you give up is independent certification. Nutricost says an accredited lab tests its products, but it does not publish who, and we could not find any Nutricost protein in the NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport registries.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="short-verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Verdict
          </h2>

          <p>
            Nutricost is a good brand for one kind of buyer: someone who wants plain, cheap protein in a big tub and does not need a certification seal. The 5 lb unflavored whey concentrate is the product most people should start with. Per the label it gives 25 g of protein, about 120 calories and 1 g of sugar in a 33 g scoop, with about 69 servings in the tub. At $97.95, the Amazon price we saw, that is about $1.42 per 25 g of protein.
          </p>

          <p>
            That price is low, but it is not in a league of its own. Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard and NOW Sports Whey Concentrate both land near $1.51 to $1.53 per 25 g in their 5 lb tubs at the prices we saw. Nutricost wins by a dime a shake, about $3 a month if you drink one a day. The bigger savings are in its casein, which works out to about $0.99 per 25 g unflavored, and its whey isolate, about $1.32 per 25 g in the 5 lb tub.
          </p>

          <p>
            The weak spot is testing. The label and the brand site say third-party tested and made in a GMP-compliant, FDA-registered facility. Nutricost does not name the lab on its product pages, and we could not find a public certificate of analysis for its protein. Several flavor and size combinations also carry a California Prop 65 notice for lead on Nutricost's own product page, which is common across protein powders but worth knowing.
          </p>

          <p>
            In short: buy Nutricost if price per gram is your main filter and you are comfortable trusting the brand's in-house quality claims. Buy something with NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport instead if you are drug-tested, or if a few extra cents per shake is worth an independent check. For every budget option ranked side by side, see our <a href="/best-cheap-protein-powder/">best cheap protein powder</a> guide.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE VERDICT</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Cheap, plain and honest on the label, not independently certified.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Start with the 5 lb unflavored whey concentrate (about $1.42 per 25 g) or the 5 lb isolate (about $1.32). For a bedtime shake, the 5 lb casein is the best value in the range. Drug-tested athletes should pay a few cents more for NOW Sports.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="lineup" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What Nutricost Sells
          </h2>

          <p>
            Nutricost is a Utah-based supplement brand best known for single-ingredient powders and capsules sold in large containers at low prices, mainly through Amazon, its own site, Walmart, Target and Vitacost. Protein is one part of a catalog that also includes creatine, collagen and dozens of vitamins. The protein line is broad and easy to navigate because almost every product comes in the same 2 lb and 5 lb sizes, with an unflavored version that is just the protein plus a small amount of sunflower lecithin for mixing.
          </p>

          <p>
            The flagship is Whey Protein Concentrate. Nutricost's site lists it in Unflavored, Vanilla, Milk Chocolate, Chocolate Peanut Butter, Strawberry Milkshake, Cookies N Cream, S'mores, Churro, Pineapple Cream, Strawberry Banana, Pumpkin Spice and a Shaq-branded Double Double Chocolate, in 1.5 lb, 2 lb and 5 lb sizes depending on flavor. Our <a href="/protein-powder/nutricost-whey-concentrate/">Nutricost Whey Concentrate label page</a> has the full unflavored panel and how it scores against other concentrates.
          </p>

          <p>
            Beside it sit Whey Protein Isolate (30 g of protein per 35 g scoop, per the label), a Grass-Fed Whey Concentrate and Grass-Fed Whey Isolate at a higher price, a Micellar Casein in Unflavored, Vanilla and Chocolate, and an Organic Pea Protein Isolate that is USDA Organic. Nutricost also sells a bone broth protein and a newer flavored pea protein, which we do not cover in detail here.
          </p>

          <p>
            Flavored Nutricost powders are sweetened with sucralose, and some, such as Milk Chocolate, add acesulfame potassium. The unflavored versions have no sweetener at all. That split matters more than flavor choice: if you avoid artificial sweeteners, buy unflavored and add your own cocoa, fruit or a little honey. Every product in the range is listed on our <a href="/brands/nutricost/">Nutricost brand page</a> with label figures side by side.
          </p>

          <p>
            One practical note on sizes: the 2 lb tubs cost noticeably more per gram than the 5 lb tubs, as the cost table below shows. If you already know you like Nutricost, the 2 lb size only makes sense for trying a new flavor or for a casein you will use a few nights a week.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Nutricost Products and Alternatives, Ranked
          </h2>

          <p>
            These are the four Nutricost tubs worth considering, ranked by how well they serve most buyers, plus one rival that costs about the same and carries a real certification. Every card links to the Amazon listing; prices are the ones we saw at the time of writing and move often, so recheck the cost per 25 g before you order. Nutrition figures come from Nutricost's labels as shown on retailer listings and vary by flavor.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="label" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. The Whey Concentrate Label, Line by Line
          </h2>

          <p>
            Three versions of the concentrate cover most purchases. The figures below are per the labels shown on Vitacost listings at the time of writing; Nutricost has revised panels before, and a Target listing shows an older or newer unflavored panel at 130 calories and 73 servings, so check the tub you receive.
          </p>

