            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying the 2 lb tub out of habit</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The 2 lb flavored concentrate costs about $1.68 per 25 g at the price we saw, against $1.42 for the 5 lb unflavored. If you already know you like Nutricost, the smaller tub costs you roughly $95 more a year at one shake a day. Use 2 lb only to try a flavor.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming the concentrate is the cheapest Nutricost whey</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Per-tub prices hide the real cost. The isolate's 30 g scoop means the 5 lb tub works out to about $1.32 per 25 g, cheaper than the concentrate at the prices we saw. Always divide by grams of protein, not by servings, before you decide which tub is the budget pick.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating "third-party tested" as a certification</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Nutricost's claim means the brand pays a lab to test its products, but it does not publish results or a registry listing. That is not the same as NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport. If you are drug-tested, buy a product you can look up in one of those public registries.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Drinking unflavored casein in water</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Micellar casein thickens and tastes chalky in plain water, which is where most negative feedback on the unflavored version comes from. Mix it with milk and cocoa, blend it with fruit, or stir it into Greek yogurt. If you will only ever shake it with water, the chocolate version is worth the extra cents.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the sodium in flavored tubs</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The unflavored concentrate lists 65 mg of sodium per scoop; the vanilla lists 300 mg. Two vanilla shakes a day add 600 mg of sodium, a meaningful share of a low-sodium diet. If you are watching salt, buy unflavored and add cinnamon, cocoa or fruit for flavor instead.
              </p>
