              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  For the price, yes. The unflavored whey concentrate is whey and sunflower lecithin with 25 g of protein per 33 g scoop per the label, and there is no sign of amino spiking. It costs about $1.42 per 25 g at the Amazon price we saw. The trade-off is testing: Nutricost says an accredited lab checks its products, but we found no NSF or Informed Sport certification.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Nutricost third-party tested?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Nutricost says its products are tested by an independent, accredited lab and made in a GMP-compliant, FDA-registered facility. It does not name the lab on its product pages or publish certificates of analysis that we could find. We also could not find Nutricost protein in the NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport registries, so treat it as brand-tested, not independently certified.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>How much protein is in Nutricost whey protein concentrate?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Per the label at the time of writing, 25 g of protein per scoop. The unflavored scoop is 33 g with about 120 calories and roughly 69 servings in the 5 lb tub. Flavored versions use a 36 g scoop with 130 calories and about 63 servings per 5 lb, because the flavoring and sweetener take up some of the weight.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Nutricost casein protein powder worth it?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  It is the best value in the Nutricost range. The unflavored 5 lb tub costs about $0.99 per 25 g of protein at the price we saw, and the chocolate about $1.17, below most whey powders. Each scoop gives 25 g of micellar casein and 580 mg of calcium per the label. Buyers find the unflavored version chalky, so most mix it with milk or flavor.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Nutricost whey isolate or concentrate better?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  The isolate has 30 g of protein per scoop, less lactose and less fat, and at the prices we saw it was cheaper per gram, about $1.32 per 25 g versus $1.42. The concentrate gets better taste and mixing feedback. If lactose bothers you or you want the lowest cost per gram, buy the isolate. If taste matters more, buy the concentrate.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Does Nutricost protein contain artificial sweeteners?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  The flavored versions do. The vanilla whey concentrate and chocolate casein list sucralose, and the milk chocolate whey adds acesulfame potassium, per the labels we checked. The unflavored whey concentrate, isolate, casein and pea protein contain no sweetener at all, so buy unflavored if you avoid artificial sweeteners and flavor the shake yourself.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Nutricost cheaper than Optimum Nutrition?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Slightly. At the Amazon prices we saw, Nutricost's 5 lb unflavored concentrate was about $1.42 per 25 g and Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard about $1.51 in its 5 lb tub. That is roughly 9 cents a shake. Gold Standard offers far more flavors and strong taste feedback, so many buyers will think the small premium is worth paying.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

